00:02:07,283 --> 00:02:09,510
this point in time. Choose the environment,

55
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:11,753
only got one. And then you could describe

56
00:02:11,753 --> 00:02:14,477
your task here. You can also choose the

57
00:02:14,477 --> 00:02:16,704
model that should handle this task. You can

58
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,283
add an image just as you can use locally and

59
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:20,693
you could kick off a task here.

60
00:02:21,302 --> 00:02:23,196
So for example, I could say change the main

61
00:02:23,228 --> 00:02:25,394
color from blue to an elegant purple.

62
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:29,308
But we can also do this from our local

63
00:02:29,324 --> 00:02:30,143
client instead.

64
00:02:30,849 --> 00:02:31,282
You can run

65
00:02:31,956 --> 00:02:35,261
/remoteenv to choose the remote environment

66
00:02:35,261 --> 00:02:36,191
you want to use.

67
00:02:36,929 --> 00:02:39,673
And once you did that, you can write a

68
00:02:39,673 --> 00:02:40,844
prompt as you always did,

69
00:02:41,582 --> 00:02:43,587
like this for example, change the main color

70
00:02:43,587 --> 00:02:45,689
from blue to an elegant purple on all UI

71
00:02:45,689 --> 00:02:48,832
components across all pages. Now this would

72
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:50,965
run locally, but you can change this by

73
00:02:51,045 --> 00:02:53,434
adding an ampersand symbol in front of it.

74
00:02:53,948 --> 00:02:55,230
And you'll see it switches to this

75
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:57,491
background mode and background means in the

76
00:02:57,491 --> 00:02:57,764
cloud.

77
00:02:58,598 --> 00:03:00,265
And you can also run multiple tasks in the

78
00:03:00,265 --> 00:03:03,039
background by describing another task here.

79
00:03:03,969 --> 00:03:06,743
Now these tasks will run in parallel in the

80
00:03:06,775 --> 00:03:09,886
cloud. So not one task, but multiple tasks

81
00:03:09,902 --> 00:03:11,987
running in parallel. Of course, you could

82
00:03:12,051 --> 00:03:14,087
also just kick them off one after another.

83
00:03:14,232 --> 00:03:17,967
You can also alternatively run Claude

84
00:03:18,624 --> 00:03:19,201
--remote

85
00:03:20,002 --> 00:03:21,157
and pass your prompt here.

86
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:25,052
With this flag added, it would not execute

87
00:03:25,052 --> 00:03:27,360
this task locally, but instead send it to

88
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:28,482
the cloud right away.

89
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,544
So as you see here, it now created a remote

90
00:03:31,544 --> 00:03:34,269
session to change this color from blue to

91
00:03:34,269 --> 00:03:36,289
purple. And I can click this link

92
00:03:36,834 --> 00:03:40,649
to see that session, also get notifications

93
00:03:40,842 --> 00:03:43,743
if I want to. And now it's working just as

94
00:03:43,743 --> 00:03:45,554
it did locally, but now in the cloud. And

95
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:47,671
what it does behind the scenes is that it

96
00:03:47,719 --> 00:03:50,526
checks out that repository, that branch,

97
00:03:51,087 --> 00:03:53,300
works on the code. It can also run tests

98
00:03:53,492 --> 00:03:55,802
there in the cloud and so on. And once it's

99
00:03:55,914 --> 00:03:57,742
done, it will create a new Git commit and

100
00:03:57,902 --> 00:04:00,067
open a pull request. So it will not just

101
